WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.525 --> 00:00:11.130
The air is still.

2
00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.191
There is a creaking sound.

3
00:00:13.811 --> 00:00:16.693
The lid of the ancient coffin slowly opens.

4
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:18.834
Everyone holds their breath.

5
00:00:19.455 --> 00:00:25.518
I gasp, then breathe a sigh of reliefㅡI see nothing but darkness inside the coffin.

6
00:00:26.318 --> 00:00:28.379
But the coffin is not empty.

7
00:00:29.656 --> 00:00:31.878
Someoneㅡor somethingㅡis in the coffin.

8
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:33.359
It is a mummy.

9
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.683
It rises with a jerky movement, and I gasp again.

10
00:00:38.443 --> 00:00:42.266
Its face and its entire body are covered loosely in bandages.

11
00:00:42.987 --> 00:00:45.349
The ragged bandages are yellow with age.

12
00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:50.173
We are frightened, but our eyes are fixed on this incredible sight.

13
00:00:50.813 --> 00:00:54.156
The mummy sits up completely and slowly turns to us.

14
00:00:54.897 --> 00:00:58.460
It begins to mutter ancient prayers that echo through the chamber.

15
00:00:59.132 --> 00:00:59.993
"What's it saying?"

16
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:02.095
I ask a fellow archeologist.

17
00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:07.179
"We must stay with him," my colleague answers, in a strange monotone.

18
00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:10.402
"We must stay with him forever."

19
00:01:11.423 --> 00:01:12.944
Did that scene sound familiar?

20
00:01:13.764 --> 00:01:20.390
It could have come from any number of horror movies: The Mummy, The Mummy's Revenge, The Mummy's Ghost,

21
00:01:20.991 --> 00:01:23.853
The Mummy's Hand, or The Mummy's Tomb.

22
00:01:24.814 --> 00:01:30.234
The mummy is a fixture in Hollywood movies, but what is the real story of the mummy?

23
00:01:30.995 --> 00:01:32.376
Where do mummies come from?

24
00:01:33.477 --> 00:01:34.458
How are they made?

25
00:01:35.519 --> 00:01:37.220
Is there really a mummy's curse?

26
00:01:38.261 --> 00:01:40.043
A mummy is a preserved corpse.

27
00:01:40.923 --> 00:01:48.850
Most mummies were made deliberately, but some mummies have been preserved accidentally due to environmental conditions.

28
00:01:49.811 --> 00:01:52.033
The earliest mummy dates back to 3300 BC.

29
00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:58.145
It is a man with red hair and is displayed in the British Museum.

30
00:01:59.086 --> 00:02:01.987
He was buried in the hot desert sand in Africa.

31
00:02:02.748 --> 00:02:07.751
The hot, dry weather dried the body and prevented the flesh from decaying.

32
00:02:08.751 --> 00:02:16.715
By 3030 BC, it was common practice in ancient Egypt to mummify the dead bodies of kings, queens,

33
00:02:17.156 --> 00:02:17.996
and the nobility.

34
00:02:18.897 --> 00:02:24.620
This practice continued for over three thousand years and produced over seventy million mummies.

35
00:02:25.628 --> 00:02:29.632
Ancient Egypt is not the only civilization to preserve its dead.

36
00:02:30.592 --> 00:02:34.916
Ancient cultures in Peru and Siberia also practiced mummification.

37
00:02:35.837 --> 00:02:41.201
People in these cultures preserved the bodies of the dead because they believed in life after death.

38
00:02:41.922 --> 00:02:46.786
They believed that a person's body must retain its physical appearance after death.

39
00:02:47.727 --> 00:02:51.730
The best way to do this was to preserve the body by mummification.

40
00:02:52.611 --> 00:03:00.648
In ancient Egypt, the mummification process took seventy days and was done by special priests who performed prayers

41
00:03:01.068 --> 00:03:02.829
at different stages in the process.

42
00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:07.351
These priests needed a detailed knowledge of human anatomy.

43
00:03:08.192 --> 00:03:13.795
The first step in mummification was removing all parts of the body that would decay quickly.

44
00:03:14.656 --> 00:03:20.018
First, a hook was stuck up the deceased person's nose and the brain was pulled out.

45
00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:25.329
This was a delicate procedure because the face could easily be disfigured.

46
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:32.433
The priest then removed the internal organs, leaving only the heart, which was believed to be the center

47
00:03:32.573 --> 00:03:33.294
of intelligence.

48
00:03:33.974 --> 00:03:35.835
The organs were preserved separately.

49
00:03:36.676 --> 00:03:45.761
The stomach, liver, lungs, and intestines were placed in special jars called canopic jars, which were later buried

50
00:03:45.961 --> 00:03:46.601
with the mummy.

51
00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:53.129
Next, the priests used a kind of salt called natron to dry out the body.

52
00:03:54.029 --> 00:03:59.512
All moisture was removed and the result was a very dried-out but recognizable human form.

53
00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:05.896
The next part in the process was to fill the sunken areas of the body with linen and

54
00:04:06.036 --> 00:04:07.037
add false eyes.

55
00:04:07.917 --> 00:04:15.401
Then, the body was covered in oil, spices, and perfume and wrapped in linen bandages coated with resin.

56
00:04:16.062 --> 00:04:18.403
Each mummy needed hundreds of yards of linen.

57
00:04:19.264 --> 00:04:26.647
The priests carefully wound long strips of linen around the body, sometimes even wrapping each finger and toe

58
00:04:26.827 --> 00:04:30.228
separately before wrapping the entire hand or foot.

59
00:04:31.249 --> 00:04:36.791
Charms were placed among the linen strips, and prayers were written on some strips for protection.

60
00:04:37.891 --> 00:04:43.634
At several stages, the body was coated with warm resin, and the wrapping resumed once again.

61
00:04:44.514 --> 00:04:48.456
When the wrapping was finished, the priests covered the body with a shroud.

62
00:04:49.248 --> 00:04:50.369
The mummy was complete.

63
00:04:51.409 --> 00:04:56.192
People have been interested in mummies for thousands of years, although their reasons have varied.

64
00:04:56.853 --> 00:05:03.296
Soon after the pharaohs were mummified and buried, thieves became very interested in their mummies because they were

65
00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:05.918
buried in tombs filled with golden treasures.

66
00:05:06.718 --> 00:05:13.622
Tomb robbing became so common that the mummies were moved to secret burial places, where some remained undisturbed

67
00:05:13.802 --> 00:05:14.923
for thousands of years.

68
00:05:15.583 --> 00:05:21.986
In the Middle Ages, it was believed that certain body parts of the mummies had medicinal properties, and

69
00:05:22.066 --> 00:05:27.208
so they were ground up into "mummy powder" for use in potions.

70
00:05:27.328 --> 00:05:34.111
In 1798, Napoleon invaded Egypt and took scientists with him to record Egyptian culture and religion.

71
00:05:34.992 --> 00:05:41.414
The research of these scientists was later published in Europe and included their investigation of Egyptian mummies.

72
00:05:42.455 --> 00:05:48.097
"Egyptology," or the study of ancient Egypt, became very popular among the upper classes.

73
00:05:49.178 --> 00:05:56.140
European aristocrats became fond of collecting mummies; they would even hold special parties at which a mummy would

74
00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:56.781
be unwrapped.

75
00:05:57.461 --> 00:06:01.503
Many nobles funded or led archeological expeditions to Egypt.

76
00:06:02.203 --> 00:06:07.285
However, most of the ancient tombs they explored had already been disturbed or robbed.

77
00:06:08.145 --> 00:06:15.260
In 1891, a young Englishman named Howard Carter arrived in Egypt and trained for many years as an

78
00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.745
archeologist. Carter was convinced that there was at least one undiscovered tomb.

79
00:06:21.566 --> 00:06:28.711
Under the patronage of Lord Carnarvon, a wealthy English aristocrat, Carter searched for tombs.

80
00:06:28.832 --> 00:06:35.497
In 1922, Lord Carnarvon summoned him to England to tell him the funding for his research had run

81
00:06:35.617 --> 00:06:40.821
out, but Carter convinced Lord Carnarvon to fund one final expedition.

82
00:06:41.722 --> 00:06:47.751
On November 4, 1922, Carter's team discovered an ancient doorway that was still sealed.

83
00:06:48.871 --> 00:06:50.813
On the doorway was the name "Tutankhamun".

84
00:06:51.854 --> 00:06:57.658
Upon discovering the untouched tomb, Howard Carter told Lord Carnarvon to come to Egypt immediately.

85
00:06:58.458 --> 00:07:01.721
When the tomb door was opened, there were many celebrations.

86
00:07:02.441 --> 00:07:09.526
They found the tomb undisturbed, and inside were many treasures, including the mummy of Pharaoh Tutankhamun and three

87
00:07:09.566 --> 00:07:11.908
gold coffins nested inside each other.

88
00:07:12.768 --> 00:07:18.013
There were rumors that Carter also found a tablet inscribed with a curse but immediately hid it from

89
00:07:18.073 --> 00:07:18.633
his workers.

90
00:07:19.614 --> 00:07:25.519
A number of mysterious deaths followed, and it was thought that Tutankhamun's mummy had cursed those who had

91
00:07:25.559 --> 00:07:26.520
disturbed his tomb.

92
00:07:27.261 --> 00:07:30.524
First, Lord Carnarvon died in Cairo of pneumonia.

93
00:07:31.325 --> 00:07:32.946
This created a media frenzy.

94
00:07:33.687 --> 00:07:36.429
News of the mummy's curse circulated around the world.

95
00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:43.403
Legend has it, that by 1929, eleven of the people connected with the discovery of the tomb had

96
00:07:43.463 --> 00:07:50.189
died, including two of Lord Carnarvon's relatives, and Carter's personal secretary, Richard Bethell.

97
00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:56.835
The bodies of the dead pharaohs, queens, and nobility of ancient Egypt weren't the only things to be

98
00:07:56.875 --> 00:08:00.539
mummifiedㅡthe ancient Egyptians also mummified animals!

99
00:08:01.359 --> 00:08:06.224
Some cults in ancient Egypt worshipped animals, like the cat goddess Bast.

100
00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.964
Thousands of cats were mummified for sale to pilgrims, who presented them as offerings to the cat goddess.

101
00:08:14.005 --> 00:08:17.747
Vast cemeteries have been discovered containing thousands of buried cats.

102
00:08:18.648 --> 00:08:23.551
It is likely that cats were bred especially to be sacrificed to the Egyptian cat goddess.

103
00:08:24.192 --> 00:08:33.578
The Egyptians believed that their gods could become certain animalsㅡcats, hawks, crocodiles, and bullsㅡand these animals were protected,

104
00:08:33.958 --> 00:08:35.940
worshipped, and mummified at death.

105
00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.862
Much of our knowledge of Egyptian mummification comes from one historian in particular: Herodotus.

106
00:08:43.782 --> 00:08:50.084
Herodotus was an ancient Greek who observed and recorded how bodies were mummified on his travels through Egypt

107
00:08:50.424 --> 00:08:51.624
in 450 BC.

108
00:08:52.945 --> 00:08:55.926
Mummification was practiced for over three thousand years.

109
00:08:56.666 --> 00:09:00.327
In 30 BC, Egypt became a province of the Roman Empire.

110
00:09:01.187 --> 00:09:07.486
The Roman presence in ancient Egypt eventually led to the decline of the practice of mummification and the

111
00:09:07.526 --> 00:09:10.387
replacement of ancient religions with Christianity.

112
00:09:11.387 --> 00:09:17.190
Nowadays, the technology of x-rays and computers allows us to examine mummies without unwrapping them.

113
00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:19.991
Their bones can be scanned and studied.

114
00:09:20.711 --> 00:09:23.332
DNA samples can be extracted and analyzed.

115
00:09:23.992 --> 00:09:27.894
Although dead for thousands of years, the mummies continue to speak to us.
